    {'para': '**107. Verbs. Accented ⟨a⟩-class.** Roots of this class form their present-stem by adding an accented ⟨a⟩ to the root, which is not gunated. The inflection of these stems is precisely like that of stems belonging to the preceding class, except as to the position of the accent; thus, ⟨kṣip⟩, present-stem ⟨kṣipa⟩, pres. ind. ⟨kṣipāmi⟩, ⟨kṣipasi⟩, ⟨kṣipati⟩, etc.'},
    {'para': '**108.** Several roots in ⟨ṛ⟩ of this class (by the Hindus written with ⟨ṝ⟩) form stems in ⟨ira⟩; e. g., ⟨kṛ⟩, ‘strew’, ⟨kirati⟩. The roots in ⟨i⟩ and ⟨u⟩ and ⟨ū⟩ change those vowels into ⟨iy⟩ and ⟨uv⟩, respectively, before the class-sign; thus, ⟨kṣi⟩, ⟨kṣiyati⟩; ⟨su⟩, ⟨suvati⟩; ⟨dhū⟩, ⟨dhuvati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**109.** For the root ⟨iṣ⟩, ‘desire’, ⟨ich⟩ is regarded as a substitute in the present-stem; thus, ⟨icchati⟩ (§ 100, note). Likewise, ⟨ṛ⟩ makes its present ⟨ṛcchati⟩; and ⟨prach⟩, sometimes given as ⟨pṛch⟩, makes ⟨pṛcchati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**110.** A number of roots following this class are strengthened in the present by a penultimate nasal; thus, ⟨sic⟩, present ind. ⟨siñcati⟩. The nasal is always assimilated in class to the following consonant; thus ⟨ñ⟩ is used before palatals, ⟨n⟩ before dentals, ⟨m⟩ before labials; and ⟨ṃ⟩ [Perry’s dotted *ṅ*, the anusvāra] before sibilants and ⟨h⟩.'},
